---
/**
 * ProcessStepsBlock — the `process_steps` layout: a centred head, a row of
 * numbered step cards, and the inline CTA that closes the band.
 *
 * EXTRACTED, NOT DESIGNED. This is the `#process` band of
 * src/pages/cosmetic-dentistry/clear-aligners.astro — `.process-grid` wrapping
 * `.process-card`, each card a `.step-tag`, a `.num`, an `<h3>` and a `<p>`,
 * followed by `.inline-cta.dark`. The class names are the page's own because
 * the page stylesheet still styles them and keeps styling them until the
 * stylesheet pass removes its copies.
 *
 * THE INLINE CTA: THE SENTENCE IS A FIELD, THE BUTTONS ARE NOT.
 * All eight pages in the family close their process band with the same card:
 * one line of copy, then "Book Online" and "Call". The two buttons are
 * identical on all eight because they are site data (src/data/contact.ts), not
 * page content, so they are written in — a per-page field for them is a
 * per-page chance to publish the wrong phone number, and the wrong phone number
 * on a dental site is a lost patient rather than a cosmetic bug. The sentence
 * IS page content: "Ready for the next step? …" here and on porcelain-veneers,
 * "Not sure which KöR level fits? …" on teeth-whitening, something else on the
 * other five. So it is `cta_text`, read here as `ctaText`.
 *
 *   `cta_text` IS BEING ADDED TO cms/mu-plugins/vs-content-model.php IN THIS
 *   SAME BATCH, AND THE REGISTRY IS WIRED IN A SEPARATE SEQUENCED STEP. Do not
 *   add `ctaText` to the selection set in manifest.ts until the PHP layout is
 *   deployed. GraphQL validates the whole document before executing any of it,
 *   so a field name the schema does not carry does not break this one band — it
 *   fails the query, and with it every page in the build.
 *
 * THE `.process-pre` MINI-GRID: TWO WIDE CARDS ABOVE THE NUMBERED ONES.
 * teeth-whitening's `#process` opens with a 2-up grid of prose cards — "What to
 * Expect During Your Teeth Whitening Appointment" and "In-Office vs At-Home KöR
 * Whitening" — sitting between the section head and the numbered steps. That is
 * 200-odd words that the backfill drops on the floor without somewhere to put
 * them, and it is not a variant of `steps`: the rows carry no tag and no
 * number, they are twice the width, and the page sheet gives them a
 * deliberately quieter treatment "so the eye still settles on the 3 primary
 * level cards". A different shape holding different content is a different
 * repeater, so `pre_cards { heading, body }`.
 *
 * The repeater is named `pre_cards` and not `cards`, `items` or `points`
 * because WPGraphQL merges two layouts that share a repeater name into one type
 * and silently drops one side's sub-fields. All three of those names are
 * already taken elsewhere in the `blocks` field.
 *
 * `layout` — the shape the steps are drawn in — offers grid, card and divided.
 * ALL THREE ARE DRAWN NOW. An unknown value still renders as a grid and says so
 * in the build log rather than rendering nothing, because a band that quietly
 * disappears is the failure mode this library is built to avoid.
 *
 *   grid     `.process-grid` > `.process-card` > step-tag, num, h3, p.
 *            The pilot's eight service pages. THE LABEL COMES FIRST.
 *   card     `.pgrid-card[data-vs-reveal]` > `.pgrid.pgrid-{n}` > `.pstep` >
 *            num, h3, p, step label. THE LABEL COMES LAST. The two hub bands:
 *            cosmetic-dentistry/index.astro:526-539 (six steps, three across)
 *            and implant-dentistry/index.astro:505-516 (four steps, four
 *            across).
 *   divided  `.vs-process-card[data-vs-reveal]` > `.vs-process-grid` >
 *            `.vs-pstep` > num, TAGLINE, h3, p, label. /services/ #process,
 *            services/index.astro:362-388, today drawn by the shared
 *            src/components/ProcessStepGrid.astro + ProcessStep.astro pair.
 *
 * THE ELEMENT ORDER IS THE WHOLE POINT, AND A WORD DIFF CANNOT SEE IT. `grid`
 * prints the step label first and `card` prints it last. Mapping a hub band as
 * `grid` would move the label to the top of ten live step cards while leaving
 * every word on the page — which is precisely the class of defect the 26
 * mis-coloured bands taught this migration to stop trusting word counts for.
 * Read the shape off the route map, never off the word count.
 *
 * `steps[].tagline` and `sub_foot` LAND WITH `card` AND `divided`.
 *   tagline  the short italic line between the numeral and the title.
 *            `divided` is the shape that ships one today (services'
 *            "Listen first." / "Calibrated to you." / "Quiet, careful
 *            delivery."). `card` prints it in the same slot when a row carries
 *            one — not because a hub band has one, but because a selected,
 *            non-empty field that renders nowhere is content silently dropped,
 *            and this library exists to not do that. `grid` prints it nowhere:
 *            that shape leads with `.step-tag` and has no extracted position
 *            for a second line, so inventing one would be design, not
 *            extraction.
 *   subFoot  cosmetic-dentistry's closing `.process-outro` paragraph
 *            (index.astro:541-544). Renders after the steps and before the CTA
 *            plate, in every shape, whenever it is non-empty. It is a bare
 *            paragraph and NOT the `ctaText` plate — that one carries two
 *            buttons, and buttons under a band that never had them is a stray
 *            control on a live client page.
 *
 * Both are additive by construction: every saved row has `tagline: null` and
 * `subFoot: null`, blank emits no element, and the three shape branches sit
 * flush against each other (`}{`) so a `grid` row gains not one byte of
 * whitespace from the two branches it does not take.
 *
 * Styles are co-located per docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 2.5, modelled on
 * FinalBand.astro: no page namespace, `--vs-*` tokens directly, band as a
 * prop, self-owned padding, zero margin.
 */
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import { phoneHref, phoneLabel, bookNowHref } from "../data/contact";
import { fillSiteTokens } from "./site-tokens";

/** One row of the layout's `steps` repeater. */
export interface ProcessStep {
  /** e.g. "Step One". */
  tag?: string | null;
  /** Text, not a number: the site prints these verbatim and some read "01". */
  num?: string | null;
  /**
   * The short italic line between the numeral and the title — "Listen first."
   * Drawn by `divided` and by `card`, never by `grid`. See the header.
   */
  tagline?: string | null;
  title?: string | null;
  body?: string | null;
}

/**
 * One row of the layout's `pre_cards` repeater — the wide prose cards above the
 * numbered steps. `heading` and not `title`: the sub-field sits beside the
 * preamble's own `heading`/`body` pair in wp-admin and matching that naming is
 * what stops an editor filling in the wrong one.
 */
export interface ProcessPreCard {
  heading?: string | null;
  body?: string | null;
}

interface Props {
  /** DOM id, already deduplicated by PageBlocks.astro. */
  id?: string;
  /** The same value as `id`. Accepted so the spread in PageBlocks is lossless. */
  anchor?: string | null;
  /** Rail label. Read by the page that builds the rail, not by this band. */
  navLabel?: string | null;
  band?: string | null;
  /**
   * The heading column's width, as a class the PAGE SHEET hooks.
   *
   * "default" (and blank) emits `class="section-head"` — every one of the
   * fifteen saved rows, byte-identical to today. "center" adds `center`.
   *
   * NOT DECORATION, AND NOT REDUNDANT WITH THIS FILE'S OWN CENTRING.
   * /new-patients/, /our-office/ and /referral-program/ style a bare
   * `.section-head` as a LEFT-aligned 60ch grid and centre it only through
   * `.section-head.center`. Dropping the class on backfill left those bands on
   * this file's own `.section-head` rule — still centred, but 760px instead of
   * 60ch, so the heading column grew by about a third and re-wrapped. Measured
   * in a browser against the built pages: 760px against 587.355px. A word diff
   * and a section-class diff are both blind to it.
   */
  headAlign?: string | null;
  eyebrow?: string | null;
  /** May contain `<em>`, and nothing else (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md, Fact 3). */
  heading?: string | null;
  body?: string | null;
  /** "grid" | "card" | "divided" — all three are drawn. See the header. */
  layout?: string | null;
  /**
   * "2" | "3" | "4" | "5" — ACF returns the select's value as a string.
   *
   * Governs `grid` and `card`. `divided` ignores it: that shape is three
   * bordered columns inside one card and nothing in src draws it at any other
   * count, so honouring the field there would be inventing a layout rather
   * than extracting one.
   */
  columns?: string | number | null;
  /**
   * The wide prose cards above the numbered steps. Empty or absent renders no
   * grid and no wrapper — see the header.
   */
  preCards?: ProcessPreCard[] | null;
  steps?: ProcessStep[] | null;
  /**
   * The closing `.process-outro` paragraph, below the steps and above the CTA
   * plate. Plain text, deliberately: cosmetic-dentistry's is one unmarked
   * sentence, and a `set:html` here would be an injection surface bought for
   * emphasis nothing uses.
   */
  subFoot?: string | null;
  /**
   * The sentence in the closing card. `<em>` and `<strong>` are permitted, as
   * they are in the eight hand-written instances this replaces. See the header
   * before adding it to the registry's selection set.
   */
  ctaText?: string | null;
}

/**
 * band value -> the modifier class the page's markup already uses.
 *
 * `paper` maps to nothing because the pilot's paper bands are a bare
 * `class="section"`. `alt` and `dark` are inherited names and inherited
 * liabilities — `.section.alt` is charcoal on porcelain-veneers.css and cream
 * on teeth-whitening.css (Fact 4) — which is why the colour below is written
 * against the band VALUE and the class is only the hook the un-migrated page
 * sheet still needs.
 */
const BAND_CLASS: Record<string, string | undefined> = {
  paper: undefined,
  cream: "cream",
  "sage-pale": "dark",
  sage: "sage",
  charcoal: "alt",
};

const {
  id,
  band = "sage-pale",
  headAlign,
  eyebrow,
  heading,
  body,
  layout = "grid",
  columns,
  preCards,
  steps,
  subFoot,
  ctaText,
} = Astro.props;

/* See the `headAlign` prop. Unknown values fall back to today's class, never to
   a band that centres itself by accident. */
const headKey = String(headAlign ?? "").trim().toLowerCase();
const headClass = headKey === "center" || headKey === "narrow" ? headKey : undefined;


const bandKey = typeof band === "string" && band !== "" ? band : "sage-pale";
const bandClass = BAND_CLASS[bandKey];

/**
 * The eyebrow carries `light` on the two bands the page spells `.section.dark`
 * — which is exactly where this band sits on the pilot, so the class is in the
 * HTML there and has to be in the HTML here. It is inert under the current
 * cascade (`.section.dark .eyebrow` at 0,3,0 already beats `.eyebrow.light` at
 * 0,2,0); it is reproduced because the phase is verified by an HTML diff, not
 * by a rendering.
 */
const eyebrowLight = bandKey === "sage-pale" || bandKey === "sage";

/**
 * The shape, resolved once, with `grid` as the value a blank field degrades to.
 *
 * `grid` is the default for the same reason it always was: it is the shape the
 * PHP select defaults to and the shape all eight pilot bands are saved as, so a
 * row that never touched the field draws what it drew before this batch.
 *
 * A choice added in wp-admin and not enumerated here reaches this line as a
 * string nothing matches. It draws as a grid and says so in the build log —
 * wrong shape, right words, loud. The alternative, keying the markup off the
 * raw value, is a band that renders nothing at all.
 *
 * `layout` arrives as a bare string: readBlocks() unwraps every one-element
 * select array once, in src/lib/page-content.ts, so there is no `Array.isArray`
 * here and there must not be one — that per-component duplication is the defect
 * that shipped two charcoal bands as paper.
 */
const SHAPES = ["grid", "card", "divided"] as const;
type Shape = (typeof SHAPES)[number];

const shape: Shape = SHAPES.includes(layout as Shape) ? (layout as Shape) : "grid";

if (layout && !SHAPES.includes(layout as Shape)) {
  console.warn(
    `[blocks] process_steps shape "${layout}" on ${Astro.url.pathname}${id ? `#${id}` : ""} is not one of ${SHAPES.join(" | ")} — drawing it as a grid`,
  );
}

/**
 * Four across is the base, and the grid carries a modifier only when it is not.
 *
 * Four matches both the PHP field's default and this page's own band, so a
 * missing value degrades to the layout the markup already has. Emitting nothing
 * at 4 is what keeps `class="process-grid"` the exact string the page ships.
 *
 * The test enumerates the values it accepts rather than passing through
 * whatever ACF hands over, so a select choice added in wp-admin and not here
 * emits no class and degrades to the 4-across base — a band one column off,
 * not a band wearing a `cols-7` no stylesheet answers. `5` is enumerated here
 * because the PHP select gained that choice in this same batch.
 */
const cols = String(columns ?? "4");
const colsClass =
  cols === "2" || cols === "3" || cols === "5" ? `cols-${cols}` : undefined;

const rows = Array.isArray(steps) ? steps : [];

/**
 * Rows with nothing in them are dropped rather than drawn.
 *
 * ACF returns a repeater an editor opened and abandoned as a row of empty
 * strings, and an empty `.process-pre-card` is a visible bordered rectangle
 * with nothing inside it. Filtering here also means a repeater of nothing but
 * blank rows leaves the grid wrapper unemitted, so the band is byte-identical
 * to one where the field was never touched.
 */
const preRows = (Array.isArray(preCards) ? preCards : []).filter(
  (c) => (c?.heading ?? "").trim() !== "" || (c?.body ?? "").trim() !== "",
);

const hasHead = Boolean(eyebrow || heading || body);
const hasCta = typeof ctaText === "string" && ctaText.trim() !== "";

/**
 * A whitespace-only `sub_foot` draws no paragraph.
 *
 * `.process-outro` carries `margin: 40px auto 0`, so an editor who put a space
 * in the field and saved would otherwise get 40px of dead air under the steps
 * with nothing in it — visible, and unattributable to any content on the page.
 */
const hasSubFoot = typeof subFoot === "string" && subFoot.trim() !== "";

/**
 * The step-count class the two hub bands already wear: `.pgrid-6` on cosmetic,
 * `.pgrid-4` on implant. It is THE NUMBER OF STEPS, NOT THE NUMBER OF COLUMNS,
 * and on those two pages the numbers disagree — cosmetic is six steps in three
 * columns (cosmetic-dentistry.css:606), implant four in four
 * (implant-dentistry.css:817). Deriving it from `columns` would emit
 * `.pgrid-3` on cosmetic, which the page sheet does not style at all, and the
 * band would fall out of its card to a single default column.
 *
 * The tracks come from `columns` instead, through the same `.cols-*` modifier
 * the grid shape uses, so one field means one thing in both shapes.
 */
const stepCountClass = `pgrid-${rows.length}`;
---

<section class:list={["section", bandClass]} id={id} data-vs-reveal-batch>
  <div class="wrap">
    {
      hasHead && (
        <div class:list={["section-head", headClass]}>
          {eyebrow && <span class:list={["eyebrow", eyebrowLight && "light"]}>{eyebrow}</span>}
          {heading && (
            <h2>
              <Fragment set:html={heading} />
            </h2>
          )}
          {body && <p>{body}</p>}
        </div>
      )
    }

    {/* Between the head and the numbered steps, which is where teeth-whitening
        puts it. `<article>` and not `<div>`: the page's own markup uses
        `<article>` here and `<div>` for the numbered cards, and these two are
        genuinely self-contained prose while a step out of its sequence is not. */}
    {
      preRows.length > 0 && (
        <div class="process-pre">
          {preRows.map((c) => (
            <article class="process-pre-card">
              {c.heading && <h3>{c.heading}</h3>}
              {c.body && <p>{c.body}</p>}
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      )
    }

    {/* THE THREE SHAPE BRANCHES ARE FLUSH — `}{`, NO NEWLINE, NO INDENT.
        Astro emits the literal text between two expression containers, so a
        blank line here would add whitespace inside `.wrap` on every band in the
        library the moment this file gained a second branch. A `grid` row saved
        before this batch has to come back byte-for-byte, and "byte" includes
        the spaces. Same idiom as GalleryMarqueeBlock.astro:190 and
        CandidacyLedgerBlock.astro:409. The gap ABOVE this comment and the gap
        BELOW the `subFoot` branch are the two that already existed and are
        deliberately left alone. And this comment container is itself flush
        against the branch below it, for the same reason. */}{
      shape === "grid" && rows.length > 0 && (
        <div class:list={["process-grid", colsClass]}>
          {rows.map((s) => (
            <div class="process-card">
              {s.tag && <span class="step-tag">{s.tag}</span>}
              {s.num && <div class="num">{s.num}</div>}
              {s.title && <h3>{s.title}</h3>}
              {s.body && <p>{s.body}</p>}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      )
    }{
      /* card — the hubs' bordered plate. `data-vs-reveal` on the plate and
         `data-vs-reveal-batch` on the section are both in the hand-written
         markup: the batch attribute staggers the band, the inner one reveals
         the card as a single object. Reproduced, not chosen.

         THE LABEL IS LAST HERE AND FIRST IN THE GRID ABOVE. That inversion is
         the reason this shape exists rather than being a `columns` variant of
         the grid. */
      shape === "card" && rows.length > 0 && (
        <div class="pgrid-card" data-vs-reveal>
          <div class:list={["pgrid", stepCountClass, colsClass]}>
            {rows.map((s) => (
              <div class="pstep">
                {s.num && <div class="pstep-num">{s.num}</div>}
                {s.tagline && <span class="pstep-tagline">{s.tagline}</span>}
                {s.title && <h3>{s.title}</h3>}
                {s.body && <p>{s.body}</p>}
                {s.tag && <span class="pstep-lbl">{s.tag}</span>}
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      )
    }{
      /* divided — /services/ #process, the three bordered columns.

         The markup is ProcessStepGrid.astro + ProcessStep.astro inlined rather
         than imported. Importing them would put their `is:global` stylesheets
         into this block's module graph, and registry.ts is imported by every
         one of the 48 routes — so two page-scale global sheets would ship on
         all of them and move every page's asset hash. The CSS below is the same
         declarations, scoped.

         `.vs-pstep-tagline` sits BETWEEN the numeral and the h3, which is where
         ProcessStep.astro puts it and nowhere a reader would guess. */
      shape === "divided" && rows.length > 0 && (
        <div class="vs-process-card" data-vs-reveal>
          <div class="vs-process-grid">
            {rows.map((s) => (
              <div class="vs-pstep">
                {s.num && <div class="vs-pstep-num">{s.num}</div>}
                {s.tagline && <span class="vs-pstep-tagline">{s.tagline}</span>}
                {s.title && <h3>{s.title}</h3>}
                {s.body && <p>{s.body}</p>}
                {s.tag && <span class="vs-pstep-lbl">{s.tag}</span>}
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      )
    }{
      /* The closing paragraph, outside the steps container in every shape —
         cosmetic-dentistry/index.astro:541 puts it after `.pgrid-card` and
         inside `.wrap`, which is exactly here.

         set:html AND the token fill, both required. /referral-program/ stores
         an inline `<a class="vs-link" href="{phone_href}">{phone}</a>` here;
         escaped, a patient would read the tag and the class attribute as body
         copy, and set:html alone would ship a link whose href is the literal
         string "{phone_href}". Both live rows that reach this line are
         markup-free prose (all-on-4-single-arch and cosmetic-dentistry's
         sedation sentence), so the switch cannot change what they draw. */
      hasSubFoot && (
        <p class="process-outro" set:html={fillSiteTokens(subFoot)} />
      )
    }

    {
      hasCta && (
        /* Always `.inline-cta.dark`: the card is a sage-deep plate on every one
           of the eight bands it appears in, and the cream `.inline-cta` variant
           in the page sheets is matched by no markup anywhere in src. The two
           buttons are site data, identical on all eight, and deliberately not
           editable — a per-page field for them is a per-page chance to publish
           the wrong phone number. */
        <div class="inline-cta dark">
          <div class="ic-text" set:html={ctaText} />
          <div class="ic-buttons">
            <Button variant="light" href={bookNowHref} hoverLabel="Let's Talk">
              Book Online
            </Button>
            <Button variant="ghost-light" href={phoneHref} hoverLabel="Tap to Dial">
              Call {phoneLabel}
            </Button>
          </div>
        </div>
      )
    }
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* ---------- band shell ----------
     `.section` owns its padding and has no margin at all, so this band cannot
     collapse into whichever band an editor drags above or below it. Same rule
     as FinalBand.astro. */
  .section {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 110px 0;
    background: var(--vs-paper);
  }
  .section.cream {
    background: var(--vs-cream);
  }
  .section.dark {
    background: var(--vs-sage-pale);
  }
  .section.sage {
    background: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  .section.alt {
    background: var(--vs-charcoal-green);
  }

  .wrap {
    max-width: 1240px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 40px;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-body);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .eyebrow.light {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }

  /* h2 resolves to ink. The page sheet reaches that colour only through a
     trailing override block whose comment says it works because of its file
     position; a block that owns its cascade states the outcome once. */
  h2 {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.022em;
    font-size: clamp(32px, 4.2vw, 54px);
  }
  /* `:global()` because the `<em>` arrives through `set:html` and carries no
     scope attribute — a plain `h2 em` compiles to `em[data-astro-cid]`, misses
     it, and drops the accent colour from every heading on the site. */
  h2 :global(em) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--vs-sage-soft);
    font-weight: 300;
  }

  p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }

  .section-head {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0 auto 64px;
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* ---------- the narrow heading column ----------
     THE CLASS ALONE DOES NOT DELIVER THIS, which is why both rules restate the
     whole box. The three sheets that use this house style put only the centring
     in `.section-head.center` and leave the width, the grid and the bottom
     margin on the bare `.section-head` — which TIES this file's own (0,2,0)
     rule and loses to bundle order. Measured, not assumed: adding the class
     moved the class attribute and left the column at 760px.

     Both rules are (0,3,0), so they beat this file's own default outright, and
     they match only rows that set `head_align` — the fifteen saved rows never
     do. Values are identical across new-patients.css:88-95,
     our-office.css:124-127 and referral-program.css:104-110; one pair of rules
     covers all three pages.

     `narrow` is the sheets' BARE `.section-head`: left-aligned. It is a real
     value and not a synonym for the default, because on those pages a plain
     `<div class="section-head">` is left-aligned at 60ch, where this file's
     default is centred at 760px — a difference no class-level sweep can see. */
  .section-head.center,
  .section-head.narrow {
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin: 0 0 56px;
  }
  .section-head.center {
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .section-head.narrow {
    text-align: left;
  }
  .section-head .eyebrow {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 14px;
  }
  .section-head .eyebrow::before,
  .section-head .eyebrow::after {
    content: "";
    width: 28px;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  .section-head h2 {
    margin: 22px 0 22px;
  }
  .section-head p {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  /* ---------- band inversions ----------
     Scoped to `.section-head`. The step cards and the closing plate are both
     surfaces of their own with colours of their own, so a band-wide inversion
     would tie on specificity with `.process-card h3` once Astro appends the
     scope attribute to both compounds, and the winner would be whichever rule
     happened to come later in this file. Scoping removes the tie instead of
     winning it — the same fault general-dentistry.css:1128 exists to patch. */
  .section.alt .section-head h2,
  .section.sage .section-head h2 {
    color: #fff;
  }
  .section.alt .section-head h2 :global(em),
  .section.sage .section-head h2 :global(em) {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }
  .section.alt .section-head p,
  .section.sage .section-head p {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
  }
  .section.alt .section-head .eyebrow,
  .section.sage .section-head .eyebrow {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }
  .section.alt .section-head .eyebrow::before,
  .section.alt .section-head .eyebrow::after,
  .section.sage .section-head .eyebrow::before,
  .section.sage .section-head .eyebrow::after {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6);
  }

  .section.dark .section-head h2 {
    color: var(--vs-ink);
  }
  .section.dark .section-head h2 :global(em) {
    color: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  .section.dark .section-head p,
  .section.dark .section-head .eyebrow {
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }
  .section.dark .section-head .eyebrow::before,
  .section.dark .section-head .eyebrow::after {
    background: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }

  /* ---------- the pre-card mini-grid ----------
     Two wide prose cards above the numbered steps. Always 2-up: the shape is
     the point of it, and it does not take `columns` — that select governs the
     numbered grid, and letting one field drive two grids would mean a band
     could not have four steps and two pre-cards, which is exactly the band this
     was extracted from.

     WHY THIS IS TWO SETS OF COLOURS AND `.process-card` IS ONE. The step card
     is a heavy translucent-white plate that reads on all five bands, so it
     needs no per-band variant. The pre-card cannot borrow that: the page sheet
     gives it a 4%-white wash specifically so "the eye still settles on the 3
     primary level cards", and 4% white over cream is nothing at all. The
     subordinate relationship is what has to survive, not the literal alpha —
     so on light bands it is a hairline outline with no fill, and on the two
     dark bands it is the page's own values to the digit. Written against the
     band VALUE rather than the class, per this file's rule, because
     `.section.alt` is charcoal on one sibling sheet and cream on another. */
  .process-pre {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 18px;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }
  .process-pre-card {
    background: transparent;
    border: 1px solid var(--vs-line);
    border-radius: var(--vs-r-lg);
    padding: 26px 28px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 10px;
    transition:
      background 0.3s ease,
      border-color 0.3s ease;
  }
  .process-pre-card:hover {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.45);
    border-color: var(--vs-sage-soft);
  }
  .process-pre-card h3 {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .process-pre-card p {
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  /* The dark-band treatment, verbatim from teeth-whitening.css:323–345 — the
     only band these cards ship on today. */
  .section.alt .process-pre-card,
  .section.sage .process-pre-card {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.04);
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14);
  }
  .section.alt .process-pre-card:hover,
  .section.sage .process-pre-card:hover {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07);
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.24);
  }
  .section.alt .process-pre-card h3,
  .section.sage .process-pre-card h3 {
    color: #fff;
  }
  .section.alt .process-pre-card p,
  .section.sage .process-pre-card p {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.78);
  }

  /* ---------- steps ---------- */
  .process-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 18px;
  }
  .process-grid.cols-3 {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  }
  .process-grid.cols-2 {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
  }
  /* Five across, and it is a scoped `.cols-5` rather than something left to the
     page sheet on purpose. porcelain-veneers.css carries
     `.pveneers .process-grid { repeat(5, 1fr) }` at (0,2,0), and this block's
     scoped `.process-grid` compiles to `.process-grid[data-astro-cid-…]`, also
     (0,2,0). Two rules at the same specificity are settled by whichever the
     bundler emits last, and bundle order is not a thing a band's column count
     should hang on — the failure is a five-step row silently drawing four wide
     and one orphaned underneath, on a live page, decided by build order. This
     compound is (0,3,0) and wins outright, so the band is deterministic whether
     the page sheet is present, absent, or removed in the stylesheet pass.

     Read off the compiled CSS rather than assumed, because it rests on
     astro.config leaving `scopedStyleStrategy` at its default `attribute`. Set
     that to `where` and the scope contributes no specificity at all: this
     compound drops to (0,2,0), the tie with `.pveneers .process-grid` comes
     back, and so does every other tie this file's scoped compounds currently
     win against a page sheet. */
  .process-grid.cols-5 {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);
  }

  /* THIS BREAKPOINT IS NOT COSMETIC — IT REPLACES ONE THE PAGE SHEET CAN NO
     LONGER APPLY.

     porcelain-veneers.css collapses its process grid to two columns at 1100px,
     but that rule is `.pveneers .process-grid` at (0,2,0) and the scoped
     `.process-grid.cols-5` above compiles to
     `.process-grid[data-astro-cid-...].cols-5` at (0,3,0). A media query adds no
     specificity, so without this rule the scoped five-track declaration wins
     inside the page's own 1100px query and the band draws FIVE across between
     992px and 1100px, where the page draws two today.

     Five 1fr tracks is the only column count in this component that needs to
     collapse before 991px, which is why no sibling `.cols-*` rule appears here:
     at 1100px a five-track row is already under 200px per card. */
  @media (max-width: 1100px) {
    .process-grid.cols-5 {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  /* The card is a translucent white plate, so it reads on every one of the five
     bands without a per-band variant — which is why there is none here even
     though the pilot only ever pairs it with sage-pale. */
  .process-card {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.7);
    border-radius: var(--vs-r-lg);
    padding: 28px 24px 30px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
    position: relative;
    min-height: 280px;
    transition:
      background 0.3s ease,
      transform 0.3s ease,
      border-color 0.3s ease;
  }
  .process-card:hover {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.75);
    border-color: #fff;
    transform: translateY(-3px);
  }
  .process-card .step-tag {
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.26em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .process-card .num {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 52px;
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .process-card h3 {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 19px;
    line-height: 1.25;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .process-card p {
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  /* ---------- the CARD shape ----------
     One paper plate with hairline-divided cells inside it, from
     cosmetic-dentistry.css:598-646 and implant-dentistry.css:810-879. The two
     sheets are the same design twice with three measured disagreements, all
     three reconciled below and named where they are reconciled.

     `.pgrid` IS AN ADDED CLASS AND A NAMED CLASS-LEVEL DELTA. The hand-written
     markup carries only `.pgrid-6` / `.pgrid-4` — the STEP COUNT — so hanging
     this CSS off that class would mean enumerating every count an editor might
     save, and an editor deleting one step would drop the band out of its own
     stylesheet. `.pgrid` is the stable hook; the count class rides along
     untouched so the page sheets that still style `.cdent .pgrid-6` keep
     working during the overlap. Expect `pgrid` on both hub bands in the
     vr-html diff, and `cols-3` on cosmetic's — deliberate, do not "fix". */
  .pgrid-card {
    background: var(--vs-paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--vs-line);
    border-radius: var(--vs-r-xl);
    padding: 14px;
    box-shadow: var(--vs-shadow);
    /* cosmetic-dentistry.css:604 states this and implant's copy does not. It
       is stated here because this block owns its own spacing and inherits none
       — the same rule the band shell follows. */
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .pgrid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 0;
    /* The inner radius has to be the outer one minus the plate's padding or
       the corner cells square off against a rounded plate. `overflow: hidden`
       is what actually clips them. */
    border-radius: calc(var(--vs-r-xl) - 14px);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .pgrid.cols-2 {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
  }
  .pgrid.cols-3 {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  }
  .pgrid.cols-5 {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);
  }

  .pstep {
    padding: 30px 28px 28px;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--vs-line);
    /* RECONCILED DRIFT 1 OF 3: cosmetic-dentistry.css:617 gives every cell a
       bottom hairline and implant-dentistry.css:824 gives none. Both sheets
       are right about their own page and the difference is not a decision —
       implant is four steps in four columns, one row, so a bottom hairline
       would have had nothing to divide and nowhere to draw. Stating it here
       and clearing it on the last row generalises both: at four columns over
       four steps every cell IS in the last row, so implant comes out with no
       bottom border, exactly as it ships today. */
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--vs-line);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
    background: var(--vs-paper);
    transition: background 0.3s ease;
    /* RECONCILED DRIFT 2 OF 3: 280px on implant (implant-dentistry.css:832),
       260px on cosmetic (cosmetic-dentistry.css:619). Keyed to the column
       count rather than averaged, because the column count is what the two
       sheets actually differ by: a four-across cell is wider, sets the same
       words in fewer lines, and needs the larger floor to keep the row even.
       Both pages therefore render at the height they render at today. */
    min-height: 280px;
    /* Nothing in either sheet positions a child against this, but both declare
       it, and a card that stops being a containing block is the kind of change
       that surfaces three components later. */
    position: relative;
  }
  .pgrid.cols-3 > .pstep {
    min-height: 260px;
  }
  .pstep:hover {
    background: var(--vs-cream);
  }

  /* The edge rules — which cell ends a row, which cells sit in the last row —
     live in the width ranges further down and NOT here. Reason in full at the
     head of that group; the short version is that "which cell is last in its
     row" changes at every breakpoint, so a rule stated once at the top has to
     be un-stated twice below it, and un-stating an `:nth-last-child()` needs a
     selector at least as specific as the one that stated it. Range-guarded
     queries state each answer exactly once instead. */

  .pstep-num {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 48px;
    line-height: 0.9;
    color: var(--vs-sage-soft);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  /* No page sheet carries this: the card shape ships no tagline today. The
     values are `.vs-pstep-tagline`'s from ProcessStep.astro, resized to this
     shape's smaller numeral and heading (48/20 against divided's 56/21) so the
     three lines keep their relative weight. */
  .pstep-tagline {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  /* Stated, not inherited. cosmetic's process band is `.section.alt` —
     charcoal — and cosmetic-dentistry.css:212-214 exists solely to pull this
     text back off white-on-white after the band-wide inversion has already hit
     it. This block's inversions are scoped to `.section-head` (see above), so
     that patch has nothing to patch here. */
  .pstep h3 {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 20px;
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .pstep p {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }
  .pstep-lbl {
    font-size: 10.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.24em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--vs-muted);
    font-weight: 500;
    /* `margin-top: auto` is what pins the label to the bottom of the cell, and
       it is the whole reason this shape prints the label last while the grid
       prints it first. */
    margin-top: auto;
    padding-top: 14px;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
  }
  .pstep-lbl::before {
    content: "";
    width: 18px;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--vs-sage);
  }

  /* RECONCILED DRIFT 3 OF 3: the two sheets stack at different widths.
     cosmetic goes three-across straight to one column at 991px
     (cosmetic-dentistry.css:941-944); implant halves four to two at 991px
     (implant-dentistry.css:1400-1412) and only reaches one column at 640px
     (implant-dentistry.css:1508-1518). Keyed to the column count again, so
     both pages break where they break today rather than one of them inheriting
     the other's breakpoints. The hairlines are re-stated at each step because
     the cell that ends a row changes when the row length does. */
  /* ---------- the card shape's width ranges ----------
     EVERY EDGE RULE IS RANGE-GUARDED — `min-width` as well as `max-width` —
     AND THAT IS NOT A STYLE PREFERENCE.

     `.pstep` carries a hairline on its right and its bottom, and each width
     then clears the ones that would draw on the outside of the plate. Which
     cells those are depends on how many columns are showing, so the answer
     changes twice on the way down. State it once at the top with
     `:nth-last-child(-n + 4)` and a phone-width one-column stack inherits it:
     four cells, all four inside "the last four", none of them with the
     divider they now need. Un-stating that needs a selector at least as heavy
     as `.pgrid:not(…):not(…):not(…) > .pstep:nth-last-child(-n + 4)`, in every
     later block, for every count — a specificity ladder that one careless
     selector silently falls off, on a live client page, as a missing hairline
     nobody diffs for.

     A range says each answer once and lets the others not apply at all.

     The three ranges are the union of the two source sheets' own, kept apart
     because the two pages break at different widths (drift 3, named above):
       >= 992    every band at its authored column count
       641-991   four- and five-across halve to two; three-across has already
                 stacked
       <= 640    one column, everywhere
     `.cols-3` alone stacks at 991 rather than 640, which is
     cosmetic-dentistry.css:941-944 exactly. */
  @media (min-width: 992px) {
    .pgrid:not(.cols-2):not(.cols-3):not(.cols-5) > .pstep:nth-child(4n) {
      border-right: 0;
    }
    .pgrid:not(.cols-2):not(.cols-3):not(.cols-5) > .pstep:nth-last-child(-n + 4) {
      border-bottom: 0;
    }
    .pgrid.cols-3 > .pstep:nth-child(3n) {
      border-right: 0;
    }
    .pgrid.cols-3 > .pstep:nth-last-child(-n + 3) {
      border-bottom: 0;
    }
    .pgrid.cols-5 > .pstep:nth-child(5n) {
      border-right: 0;
    }
    .pgrid.cols-5 > .pstep:nth-last-child(-n + 5) {
      border-bottom: 0;
    }
  }

  /* Two across from 641px up: the four- and five-across bands in the middle
     range, and `.cols-2` at every width above the stack. One pair of edge
     rules serves all three because in this range they are all two columns. */
  @media (min-width: 641px) {
    .pgrid.cols-2 > .pstep:nth-child(2n) {
      border-right: 0;
    }
    .pgrid.cols-2 > .pstep:nth-last-child(-n + 2) {
      border-bottom: 0;
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 641px) and (max-width: 991px) {
    .pgrid:not(.cols-2):not(.cols-3):not(.cols-5),
    .pgrid.cols-5 {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
    .pgrid:not(.cols-2):not(.cols-3):not(.cols-5) > .pstep:nth-child(2n),
    .pgrid.cols-5 > .pstep:nth-child(2n) {
      border-right: 0;
    }
    .pgrid:not(.cols-2):not(.cols-3):not(.cols-5) > .pstep:nth-last-child(-n + 2),
    .pgrid.cols-5 > .pstep:nth-last-child(-n + 2) {
      border-bottom: 0;
    }
  }

  /* Three across stacks a full breakpoint earlier than the rest — three
     columns of a six-step band is already a ~24ch measure at 991px, which is
     the call cosmetic-dentistry.css:941 makes and the reason its band does not
     wait for 640. `min-height` goes to 0 in the same breath: a 260px floor
     under a one-column stack is six tall boxes of white space. */
  @media (max-width: 991px) {
    .pgrid.cols-3 {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .pgrid.cols-3 > .pstep {
      border-right: 0;
      min-height: 0;
    }
    .pgrid.cols-3 > .pstep:last-child {
      border-bottom: 0;
    }
  }

  /* One column, everything.
     THE MODIFIERS ARE ENUMERATED BESIDE THE BASE for the reason the grid
     shape's 780px block already documents: `.pgrid` scoped is (0,2,0) and
     `.pgrid.cols-5` is (0,3,0), a media query adds no specificity, and a
     modifier left off this list keeps its desktop track count onto a phone. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .pgrid,
    .pgrid.cols-2,
    .pgrid.cols-5 {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .pgrid > .pstep {
      border-right: 0;
      min-height: 0;
    }
    .pgrid > .pstep:last-child {
      border-bottom: 0;
    }
  }

  /* ---------- the DIVIDED shape ----------
     Copied declaration-for-declaration from src/components/ProcessStepGrid.astro
     and src/components/ProcessStep.astro, which is what /services/ #process
     draws through today. Copied and not imported: both of those components
     publish their CSS with `is:global`, and registry.ts reaches all 48 routes,
     so importing them would ship two page-scale global sheets site-wide and
     move every page's asset hash. Class names are theirs to the letter so the
     shape stays one shape while both spellings exist.

     Three columns, fixed. `columns` does not reach this shape — see the prop. */
  .vs-process-card {
    background: var(--vs-paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--vs-line);
    border-radius: var(--vs-r-xl);
    padding: 14px;
    box-shadow: var(--vs-shadow);
  }
  .vs-process-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 0;
    border-radius: calc(var(--vs-r-xl) - 14px);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .vs-pstep {
    padding: 36px 32px;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--vs-line);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
    background: var(--vs-paper);
    transition: background 0.3s ease;
  }
  /* `:last-child` and not `:nth-child(3n)` because this grid is three columns
     at every width and a fourth step would wrap — which the shared component
     does not handle either. A divided band is three steps; more than three is
     a `card` band. */
  .vs-process-grid > .vs-pstep:last-child {
    border-right: 0;
  }
  .vs-pstep:hover {
    background: var(--vs-cream);
  }
  .vs-pstep-num {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 56px;
    color: var(--vs-sage-soft);
    line-height: 0.9;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .vs-pstep-tagline {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 16px;
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .vs-pstep h3 {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 21px;
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .vs-pstep p {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }
  .vs-pstep-lbl {
    font-size: 10.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.24em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--vs-muted);
    margin-top: auto;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
    padding-top: 16px;
  }
  .vs-pstep-lbl::before {
    content: "";
    width: 18px;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  @media (max-width: 991px) {
    .vs-process-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .vs-process-grid > .vs-pstep {
      border-right: 0;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--vs-line);
    }
    .vs-process-grid > .vs-pstep:last-child {
      border-bottom: 0;
    }
  }

  /* ---------- the closing paragraph ----------
     cosmetic-dentistry.css:647-651, with the alt-band colour from :1008. The
     colour is written against the band VALUE rather than the class, per this
     file's rule, and it is needed here where it was not needed on the step
     cards: the outro is bare text on the band itself, not text on a paper
     plate, so it is the one element in this shape the band can reach. */
  .process-outro {
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin: 40px auto 0;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .section.alt .process-outro,
  .section.sage .process-outro {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
  }

  /* ---------- closing plate ---------- */
  .inline-cta {
    margin-top: 44px;
    background: var(--vs-cream);
    border: 1px solid var(--vs-line);
    border-radius: var(--vs-r-lg);
    padding: 28px 32px;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 30px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .inline-cta .ic-text {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(18px, 1.8vw, 22px);
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    line-height: 1.35;
    max-width: 46ch;
  }
